WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(cheerful music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.340
- [Narrator] Party
leaders are still debating

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:03.480
raising the debt ceiling

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.630
as the deadline to default gets closer.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.820
But they do agree on one thing.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.040
- Defaulting on our debt is not an option.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.543
- Not an option.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.650
It will have catastrophic consequences.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.690
- [Narrator] They're not exaggerating.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.540
The US Treasury uses tax revenue and debt

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.550
to pay the country's bills

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.520
like funding government
programs, social security,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.070
and paying interest on existing debt.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.080
If it runs out of money and isn't able

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.940
to pay these investors back,

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.590
that's default,

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.090
and that's what will happen if Congress

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.370
doesn't raise the debt
ceiling to allow the Treasury

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.780
to take on more debt.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.720
- Which would cause massive
upheaval in financial markets.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.360
Stock prices falling,
interest rates rising,

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.730
probably the dollar falling too

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.220
and that could affect a wide range

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.350
of your own financial life.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:53.610
In a worse case scenario,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.710
it could cause a whole new recession.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.480
- [Narrator] It's because
US debt has become

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.130
the core of the economy

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.040
from Wall Street to
banks to interest rates.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.070
- US Treasury borrowing
is the center of gravity

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.860
for almost all finance on the globe.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.600
- [Narrator] Here's why, how it works

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.973
and what a default would mean for you.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.070
This is what the us debt
is made out of, bonds.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.800
Well, technically they could
also be a bill or a note

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.980
but for our purposes, we're
going to call them all bonds.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:23.813
And they're not just how

00:01:23.813 --> 00:01:25.710
the federal government takes out debt.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.400
Bonds can be given out by
cities, banks, corporations

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.430
because a bond is basically an I owe you.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.400
An investor buys say $1,000 bond.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.660
The government or business

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.300
pays them interest on it in installments,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.600
and at the end of the
term, their $1,000 back.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.250
And that amount of interest is based

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.790
on how risky that bond seems to investors.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.490
Companies that have a long
history of paying their debts,

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.390
say Microsoft, pay a lower
interest to bond holders.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.850
The bank, Credit Suisse, paid nearly 10%

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:00.000
on some riskier bonds
before those were wiped out

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.460
when the collapsing bank was taken over.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.130
The higher the risk,
the higher the reward.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.350
- Investors are always calculating

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.150
what's the extra return I want

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.860
for the extra risk I'm taking?

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:11.970
A lot of investors will ask

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.930
what is the return I would get
if there was no risk at all?

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.400
And their answer to that question

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.620
has always been to look
at the US Treasury Bond.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.710
The US Treasury is seen as the safest

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:26.640
and soundest borrower on planet Earth.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.920
- [Narrator] Which is why
it's considered a benchmark

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.420
for all investments.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.120
- A benchmark is really the base off

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.420
of which a lot of other borrowing is done.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.210
A lender will wanna have something larger

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:40.170
than that benchmark.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.900
That's considered to be
the lowest possible rate.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.750
If the US Treasury is issuing at 4%,

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.701
then corporate bonds or mortgage bonds

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:51.840
or other governments
might issue it 5% or 6%.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.730
- [Narrator] You can see
that benchmark treasury rate

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.560
here in this chart.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.560
Now here's the 30 year mortgage rate.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.570
Here's a grouping of AAA rated

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.390
considered the safest corporate bonds.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:06.420
They go up and down in
relation to the US debt,

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.510
which is always the lowest, the benchmark

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.520
- It's kind of like the sun.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.400
Everything revolves around that benchmark,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.320
that US treasury rate.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.510
- [Narrator] It's more than
just the rate of the bond,

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.690
it's the bonds themselves
that are so central.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.360
Wall Street firms, banks, companies,

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.280
a lot of financial institutions

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.710
keep money in US treasury bonds.

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.450
- It's just always expected

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:34.260
that the US Treasury is
gonna make good on its debt.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.910
- [Narrator] Bonds could
be affected two ways

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.440
by this debt ceiling debate.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.600
A contentious fight could
make investors worry

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.850
they may not get their money back on time,

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:43.740
and as a result, they would most likely

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.590
demand a higher interest
rate for their risk.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.200
The same could happen if the
big three credit rating firms

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.310
have the same fear and downgrade
the US AAA credit rating.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:54.450
It's happened before.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.270
Standard and Poor's did that in 2011,

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.490
citing the fight around the debt ceiling.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:00.660
But they were the only ones

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.480
and it didn't have much
of an effect back then.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.620
The worst case scenario

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.900
is if Congress doesn't
raise the debt ceiling

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.780
and the US is unable to
pay investors on time

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.478
and actually defaults.

00:04:11.478 --> 00:04:13.230
(ringer buzzing)

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.630
Then US bonds, the benchmark,

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.900
would be proven unsafe investments.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:20.940
If the value of the bonds go down,

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.850
these financial portfolios would too.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.220
Investors would demand
higher interest rates

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.050
for their higher risk

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:31.140
and that would raise
interest rates for everyone.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.480
- It's kind of like being hit by a meteor.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.590
When you don't have confidence

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.080
that the benchmark itself
is gonna be paid off,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.150
it creates uncertainty
in financial markets.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.410
When there's financial chaos,

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.660
very often companies lay people off.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.580
So everybody has a stake in this.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:45.870
- [Narrator] An analysis by Moody's

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.940
looked at the different scenarios.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.320
If the default is brief

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.080
and the Treasury
prioritizes paying investors

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.840
but not say senior social security,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.320
the already fragile economy
would have a mild recession

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.080
with close to a million jobs lost.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.650
If the default goes on for
weeks and investors aren't paid,

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.550
the economic downturn would
be comparable to 2008,

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:10.800
costing more than 7 million jobs

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.403
and wiping out $10 trillion
in household wealth.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.930
To avoid both scenarios, the
House, Senate and White House

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.850
must agree to raise the debt ceiling

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.590
before the June 1st deadline.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:23.880
Many experts call it,

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:24.960
- The game of chicken.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.820
- [Narrator] But a game where we all lose

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.383
if they don't raise it
before the clock runs out.

00:05:29.383 --> 00:05:32.133
(cheerful music)

